1
00:00:02,500 --> 00:00:06,842
Right now, a machine built in 1977 is still sending

2
00:00:06,842 --> 00:00:10,759
us messages from more than twenty-four billion

3
00:00:10,759 --> 00:00:12,121
kilometres away.

4
00:00:13,537 --> 00:00:16,601
And on Mars, another machine has said nothing

5
00:00:16,601 --> 00:00:18,166
at all for eight years.

6
00:00:19,832 --> 00:00:23,779
Same agency. Same promise. Two very different endings.

7
00:00:25,444 --> 00:00:27,385
We're a team from Bangladesh.

8
00:00:28,801 --> 00:00:32,023
We're a developer, a researcher, a designer and

9
00:00:32,023 --> 00:00:32,982
a storyteller.

10
00:00:34,648 --> 00:00:37,940
We kept asking one question: when a mission goes

11
00:00:37,940 --> 00:00:40,067
quiet, where does its story go?

12
00:00:41,732 --> 00:00:44,697
Our answer is a website called Abandoned,

13
00:00:44,697 --> 00:00:45,999
But Not Forgotten.

14
00:00:46,798 --> 00:00:50,997
Since the 1960s, NASA has left hardware on the

15
00:00:50,997 --> 00:00:54,009
Moon, on Mars, and in deep space.

16
00:00:55,165 --> 00:00:58,452
The facts exist. But they're scattered across

17
00:00:58,452 --> 00:01:01,373
mission pages, press releases, and PDFs.

18
00:01:02,529 --> 00:01:05,686
And one word hides the truth: “abandoned.”

19
00:01:06,842 --> 00:01:09,800
Nobody drove these machines home. They worked

20
00:01:09,800 --> 00:01:11,706
until something stopped them.

21
00:01:12,612 --> 00:01:15,599
Dust. Cold. Fading power. Distance.

22
00:01:16,755 --> 00:01:20,207
Opportunity was built to last ninety Martian days.

23
00:01:20,207 --> 00:01:22,899
It worked for more than fourteen years.

24
00:01:24,055 --> 00:01:28,962
Then, in 2018, a global dust storm blocked its sunlight.

25
00:01:29,868 --> 00:01:33,740
Engineers sent more than a thousand recovery commands.

26
00:01:33,740 --> 00:01:35,031
Nothing came back.

27
00:01:36,187 --> 00:01:39,489
That's the part that matters most: why a mission

28
00:01:39,489 --> 00:01:42,310
ended. And it's the part hardest to find.

29
00:01:43,466 --> 00:01:46,751
A student shouldn't need ten tabs to understand it.

30
00:01:47,551 --> 00:01:51,688
So we built one place for it. Fifteen missions,

31
00:01:51,688 --> 00:01:54,857
from 1966 to today, across the Moon,

32
00:01:54,857 --> 00:01:56,618
Mars and deep space.

33
00:01:57,318 --> 00:02:00,808
Scroll the homepage, and the story unfolds scene

34
00:02:00,808 --> 00:02:04,443
by scene, from leaving Earth to the final silence.

35
00:02:05,143 --> 00:02:07,645
Follow six decades on the timeline,

36
00:02:07,645 --> 00:02:10,647
or find every machine on the hardware map.

37
00:02:11,347 --> 00:02:15,165
Our Final Signal page separates four moments most

38
00:02:15,165 --> 00:02:18,671
people mix up: launch, arrival, last contact,

39
00:02:18,671 --> 00:02:20,757
and official closure.

40
00:02:20,757 --> 00:02:24,064
Because “lost” and "finished" are not the same thing.

41
00:02:24,764 --> 00:02:27,964
Pick a spacecraft and turn it around in 3D.

42
00:02:28,664 --> 00:02:32,123
Or play Mission Detective. Read the clues about

43
00:02:32,123 --> 00:02:34,698
Spirit, Opportunity, or Surveyor 3,

44
00:02:34,698 --> 00:02:36,685
and work out what happened.

45
00:02:37,385 --> 00:02:40,069
There's a quiz, a mission comparison,

46
00:02:40,069 --> 00:02:41,374
and a 3D game too.

47
00:02:42,074 --> 00:02:46,385
Every fact comes from NASA and JPL sources.

48
00:02:46,385 --> 00:02:49,553
And when a machine “speaks” in first person,

49
00:02:49,553 --> 00:02:52,937
we label it clearly as creative interpretation,

50
00:02:52,937 --> 00:02:55,025
never as a real transmission.

51
00:02:55,825 --> 00:02:57,361
Why does this matter?

52
00:02:58,061 --> 00:03:01,333
For a student, it turns space history from a list

53
00:03:01,333 --> 00:03:04,404
of dates into a set of stories with an ending,

54
00:03:04,404 --> 00:03:05,272
and a reason.

55
00:03:05,972 --> 00:03:10,177
It's built with plain HTML, CSS, and JavaScript.

56
00:03:10,177 --> 00:03:15,446
No framework, no backend, and most of it works offline.

57
00:03:15,446 --> 00:03:18,514
So a classroom with a weak connection can still

58
00:03:18,514 --> 00:03:20,865
open it. That matters where we live.

59
00:03:21,565 --> 00:03:24,504
And it's honest. Sources are on the site,

60
00:03:24,504 --> 00:03:28,733
and the line between fact and imagination is never blurred.

61
00:03:29,433 --> 00:03:32,418
This is a first version, with fifteen missions.

62
00:03:32,418 --> 00:03:35,472
To take it further, we need three things:

63
00:03:35,472 --> 00:03:38,695
experts to review and expand the archive,

64
00:03:38,695 --> 00:03:43,284
access to more NASA imagery and 3D surface data,

65
00:03:43,284 --> 00:03:46,297
and a Bangla version, with teacher resources,

66
00:03:46,297 --> 00:03:48,105
so schools here can use it.

67
00:03:50,205 --> 00:03:54,282
Voyager is still talking. Opportunity went quiet.

68
00:03:54,282 --> 00:03:57,484
Our job is to make sure both are heard.

69
00:03:57,484 --> 00:03:59,596
Abandoned, But Not Forgotten.
